content.text:
The drawing shows a central carbon with three substituents:
- a double bond to a terminal CH2, drawn as the two lines running to the lower right;
- a CO2Me group (methyl ester), drawn straight up;
- an "NC" label at the lower left, attached to the central carbon through its C atom.

The label "NC" is written with N first, but the bond runs from the C end toward the central carbon, so the group is a nitrile (–C≡N), not an isocyanide. This is methyl 2-cyanoacrylate.

SMILES: C=C(C#N)C(=O)OC
